%N A270840 Numbers k such that k^3 + 1 is a 2-pseudoprime (or Sarrus number).
%C A270840 Since k^3 + 1 is clearly composite (k>1), this is k such that 2^(k^3) == 1 (mod k^3+1).
%C A270840 There is only one k such that k + 1 is a composite number up to 10^10: 14700. - _Altug Alkan_, Mar 27 2016
%F A270840 a(n) = A265653(n) - 1. - _Altug Alkan_, Mar 24 2016
%t A270840 q[k_] := CompositeQ[k^3 + 1] && PowerMod[2, k^3, k^3 + 1] == 1; Select[Range[10^6], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 26 2026 *)
%o A270840 (PARI) is(n)=Mod(2,n^3+1)^(n^3)==1
%Y A270840 Cf. A001567, A135590, A265653.
